<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dormition Cathedral, Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1479">1479</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Cathedral of the Dormition, Moscow, Assumption Cathedral, Moscow, Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dormition Cathedral is the principal church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a>, rebuilt in 1475–1479 by the Italian architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotele-fioravanti">Aristotele Fioravanti</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> on the model of the cathedral at Vladimir. It served as the mother church of Muscovy and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, where metropolitans and patriarchs were installed and where rulers were crowned, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in 1547 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow.jpg" alt="Dormition Cathedral, Moscow" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Kruczynski · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dormition_Cathedral,_Moscow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
